  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Diagnosing a Color Parsing Error**\n\nI\'m currently focused on a color parsing error related to the \"oklch\" function. It seems this is common when using `jspdf` or `html2canvas`, and Tailwind CSS v4\'s reliance on `oklch` is likely the culprit. Investigating the compatibility between these libraries is the current priority.\n\n\n**Addressing Color Conversion Issues**\n\nI\'ve discovered that `html2canvas` doesn\'t support `oklch`, which is causing issues with Tailwind v4 color outputs. My current focus is resolving the color parsing problem. I am going to try inline styles, and standard hex/rgb colors, and a custom override to avoid `oklch` altogether in the hidden layout elements. However, even simple styles might be affected by inherited Tailwind properties, so I\'m investigating how to comprehensively bypass this.\n\n\n**Evaluating Printing Alternatives**\n\nI\'m now reconsidering the necessity of `html2canvas`. The user requires PDF downloads, and `jsPDF` might directly generate the PDF using its text APIs. This seems simpler and more efficient for text-heavy documents. I\'m investigating if we can bypass `html2canvas` altogether, potentially using `window.print()` or directly building the PDF.\n\n\n**Implementing PDF Generation**\n\nI\'m now focused on directly building the \"Question Sheet\" and \"Markscheme\" PDFs using `jsPDF`. The plan is to rewrite `generatePDF` to utilize `pdf.text()`, `pdf.line()`, and other primitives, completely bypassing `html2canvas` and its color issues. I\'ve begun experimenting with `jsPDF` for the \"Question Sheet,\" with sample code for generating text. I\'m tackling generating dotted lines within the `jsPDF` context, for answer spaces.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 27
      nanos: 738618800
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
